revalidate = 3600
— cached at the edge, rebuilt hourly
Key Takeaways
SQLite is production-ready for read-heavy, single-server applications. Our 370-challenge dataset loads in under 5ms.
Server Components + Client interactivity is the sweet spot. Fetch data server-side, calculate client-side. Best of both worlds.
True cost > listed price. If you're building any comparison tool, think about what "cost" really means to your users. The compound metric (cost/funded capital) revealed insights that raw prices obscured.
i18n from day one. Retrofitting 10 locales is painful. We started with
next-intl
early and now serve traders in their native language across 10 locales.SQLite + Docker needs care. Mount the directory, not the file. Never access from both host and container. Use WAL mode but respect its constraints.
The True Cost Calculator is live at propfirmkey.com/en/tools/true-cost-calculator — processing 370+ challenges from 35 firms with real-time discount calculations. If you're building fintech tools with Next.js, I hope this architectural breakdown was useful.
The full platform at PropFirm Key covers firm comparisons, detailed reviews, and the latest discount codes for prop trading challenges.
Built with Next.js 16, TypeScript, SQLite, Drizzle ORM, and Tailwind CSS. Deployed on Docker with Caddy reverse proxy.

Continuing our journey with KCL, in this post we will explore how to accelerate the creation of reusable Kubernetes packages from CRDs using KIRO and the AMDF MCP tool.
Kiro + AMDF MCP elevate a process that traditionally requires long times in a conversation of minutes, where the developer simply indicates what resources are needed.
Additionally, by using KCL instead of traditional YAML, KCL provides strong typing, validation at development time, and abstraction capabilities that transform repetitive configurations into reusable and maintainable code. The result is infrastructure as data that is not only generated quickly, but is also robust, validated, and ready."
Prerequisites
- Kubernetes cluster
- CRDS in Kubernetes Cluster, for this demo ACK, KRO and External Secrets Operator
- AMDF
- KIRO
- KCL
Step 1.
Validate your CRDS.
In this case the cluster have CRDS from ACK EC2 Controllers.
Step 2.
Connect MCP.
With AMDF installed is so simple like as execute
amdf mcp-server
Starting AMDF MCP Server...
Step 3.
With the MCP Server running, open KIRO for start to create reusable packages using a basic prompt.
Kiro, I need to create a ResourceGraphDefinition CRD KCL schema for generate an abstraction of multiple resources.
Step 4.
With the prompt running, the AMDF MCP tools start to working.
Step 5.
AMDF Automatically create this directorys using KCL sintax.
Step 6.
For this demo, I migrated a code from crossplane to KRO, using
helper functions and CEL expretions required for KRO.
"""
Example: Using ResourceGraphDefinition
This file demonstrates how to use the generated blueprint with optional policy validation.
"""
# ============================================================================
# MIGRACIÓN DE KEYCLOAK DE CROSSPLANE A KRO
# Versión con funciones helper y expresiones CEL
# ============================================================================
import blueprints
# ============================================================================
# SECCIÓN 1: FUNCIONES HELPER
# ============================================================================
# Las funciones helper son plantillas reutilizables que construyen estructuras
# de datos. Reciben parámetros (incluyendo expresiones CEL) y devuelven objetos.
_buildPostgresContainer = lambda image: str, secretName: str -> any {
{
name = "postgres"
image = image
ports = [{
containerPort = 5432
}]
env = [
{
name = "POSTGRES_DB"
value = "keycloak"
}
{
name = "POSTGRES_USER"
value = "keycloak"
}
{
name = "POSTGRES_PASSWORD"
valueFrom = {
secretKeyRef = {
name = secretName
key = "password"
}
}
}
]
}
}
_buildServiceTemplate = lambda name: str, selector: {str:str}, port: int -> any {
{
apiVersion = "v1"
kind = "Service"
metadata = {
name = name # Puede incluir expresiones CEL
namespace = "default"
}
spec = {
selector = selector
ports = [{
port = port
targetPort = port
protocol = "TCP"
}]
type = "ClusterIP"
}
}
}
_includeWhenLocal = lambda isLocal: bool -> [str] {
[r"${schema.spec.localTest}"] if isLocal else [r"${!schema.spec.localTest}"]
}
_tagsTemplate = lambda name: str, projectName: str -> [any] {
[
{
key = "Name"
value = name
}
{
key = "Project"
value = projectName
}
]
}
blueprints.ResourcegraphdefinitionBlueprint {
_metadataName = "keycloak-stack"
_schema = {
apiVersion = "v1alpha1"
kind = "KeycloakStack"
group = "kro.run"
# Campos del spec (lo que el usuario proporciona en el claim)
spec.projectName = "string | default=demo"
spec.environment = "string | default=dev" # dev = sin certificado (HTTP), prod = con certificado (HTTPS)
spec.keycloakMode = r"""string | default="start-dev" """ # start-dev o start (producción)
spec.keycloakReplicas = "integer | default=1"
spec.keycloakImage = r"""string | default="quay.io/keycloak/keycloak:latest" """
spec.keycloakAdminUser = "string | default=admin"
spec.keycloakHostname = "string | required=true"
spec.certificateSecretName = "string"
spec.postgresImage = r"""string | default="postgres:15" """
spec.postgresPassword = "string | required=true"
# Configuración de producción
spec.kcProxy = r"""string | default="" """ # forwarded, xforwarded, o vacío para deshabilitar (desarrollo local)
spec.kcHttpEnabled = r"""boolean | default=true""" # Permitir HTTP interno cuando proxy=edge
spec.resourceRequestsCpu = r"""string | default="500m" """
spec.resourceRequestsMemory = r"""string | default="1Gi" """
spec.resourceLimitsCpu = r"""string | default="2000m" """
spec.resourceLimitsMemory = r"""string | default="2Gi" """
###### RDS.
spec.localTest = r"""boolean | required=true"""
spec.rdsInstanceClass = r"""string | default="db.t3.micro" """
spec.rdsAllocatedStorage = r"""integer | default=20"""
spec.rdsEngineVersion = r"""string | default="15" """
spec.rdsDBName = r"""string | default="keycloak" """
spec.rdsUsername = r"""string | default="keycloak" """
# Opciones para manejar el password de RDS:
# Opción 1: Password directo
spec.rdsPassword = "string"
# Opción 2: Referenciar un Secret existente
spec.rdsPasswordSecretName = "string"
spec.rdsPasswordSecretKey = r"""string | default="password" """
# Opción 3: AWS Secrets Manager
spec.rdsManageMasterUserPassword = r"""boolean | default=false"""
spec.rdsSubnetGroupName = "string"
spec.rdsSubnetIDs = "[]string"
spec.rdsVPCID = "string"
spec.rdsSecurityGroupIDs = "[]string"
spec.rdsAllowedCIDRs = "[]string"
}
_resources = [
{
id = "postgresSecret"
